// The save-transfer and device-sync Worker (GitHub issue #276). Run it locally with `pnpm dev:sync` (no Cloudflare account needed),
// deploy with `pnpm --filter @mc/sync-worker deploy` (production) or `... deploy --env staging`.
//
// Free-plan budget: 100,000 Worker requests per day, and Durable Objects are free only when SQLite-backed (hence
// `new_sqlite_classes` below), with their own daily caps on requests and on rows read and written. A transfer costs a
// few of each (one upload, one fetch, one alarm), far under the caps for a hobby game. If the Worker is ever abused,
// set TRANSFERS_ENABLED to anything but "true" and redeploy: every /transfer route answers 503 at once.
//
// A flood still spends quota: each request costs one Worker request plus 1-3 Durable Object requests, even when the
// caller is blocked. TRANSFERS_ENABLED is the response to a flood.
//
// Device sync (/sync, one SyncSpace Durable Object per space) costs, in rows written (the free plan allows 100,000 a
// day, and rows read 5 million):
//   - a batch writes 1 row for the space's meta, plus 2 per accepted op that creates a record and 3 per op that rewrites
//     or deletes one (the record's stub, its new version-index row, and the old index row removed); a conflicting op
//     writes nothing. A request that only reads writes 2 rows (meta and alarm) once a day, else none.
//   - every sync request also writes about 2 rows in the caller's RateLimiter object (count, then refund).
//   - reads: a batch reads the meta and one stub per distinct key; a changes page reads the meta and each record it
//     returns.
// A typical session is one changes pull and one push of a few saves: say 3 rewritten records is 1 + 3 x 3 = 10 rows plus
// 4 for the two requests' limiter rows, about 15 rows and 6 Durable Object requests. At 10 pushes a day that is 150
// rows per active player, so the 100,000-row cap is about 600 active players a day (the Worker request cap, 100,000,
// is not close). Each sync request is 3 Durable Object requests (limiter twice, space once) against their own daily
// cap.
//
// Worst case: each IP is held to 5,000 rows of writes per day (the spaces' rows plus the limiter's own, counted as the
// space reports them), so the account's 100,000 rows are spent by about 19 IPs that each use their whole allowance
// (the last request may overshoot by a batch, at most 301 rows); a flood from many addresses is what SYNC_ENABLED is
// for. One IP can also make 3 new spaces a day, 300 requests per 10 minutes, and a space takes 60 batches and 1,500 ops
// an hour. A space holds at most MAX_SPACE_BYTES (10 MiB, counting encoded data and 64 bytes per record row) and is
// deleted after a year unused. Set a TURNSTILE_SECRET to require the human check on creating a space too.
// SYNC_ENABLED is the sync kill switch, as TRANSFERS_ENABLED is the transfer one: anything but "true" makes every /sync
// route answer 503.
//
// Optional bot check: `wrangler secret put TURNSTILE_SECRET` (add `--env staging` for staging). It is a secret, not a
// var, so it is not listed below; when it is set, a transfer upload and a sync space creation must carry a valid CF-Turnstile-Response header.
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "mc-sync",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-10-01",

  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "TRANSFER_ROOM", "class_name": "TransferRoom" },
      { "name": "RATE_LIMITER", "class_name": "RateLimiter" },
      { "name": "SYNC_SPACE", "class_name": "SyncSpace" },
    ],
  },
  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["TransferRoom", "RateLimiter"] },
    { "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["SyncSpace"] },
  ],

  "vars": {
    "TRANSFERS_ENABLED": "true",
    "MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES": "2097152",
    "SYNC_ENABLED": "true",
    "MAX_SPACE_BYTES": "10485760",
    // Comma list; a `*` in the host matches a name (or, after a colon, a port). Only these origins get CORS headers.
    // Netlify previews and aliases, local dev servers, and the Capacitor and Tauri webviews.
    "ALLOWED_ORIGINS": "https://marvel-champions-digital-game.netlify.app,https://*--marvel-champions-digital-game.netlify.app,http://localhost:*,http://127.0.0.1:*,https://localhost,capacitor://localhost,tauri://localhost,http://tauri.localhost,https://tauri.localhost",
  },

  // Wrangler environments do not inherit bindings or vars, so staging repeats them.
  "env": {
    "staging": {
      "name": "mc-sync-staging",
      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [
          { "name": "TRANSFER_ROOM", "class_name": "TransferRoom" },
          { "name": "RATE_LIMITER", "class_name": "RateLimiter" },
          { "name": "SYNC_SPACE", "class_name": "SyncSpace" },
        ],
      },
      "migrations": [
        { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["TransferRoom", "RateLimiter"] },
        { "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["SyncSpace"] },
      ],
      "vars": {
        "TRANSFERS_ENABLED": "true",
        "MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES": "2097152",
        "SYNC_ENABLED": "true",
        "MAX_SPACE_BYTES": "10485760",
        "ALLOWED_ORIGINS": "https://marvel-champions-digital-game.netlify.app,https://*--marvel-champions-digital-game.netlify.app,http://localhost:*,http://127.0.0.1:*,https://localhost,capacitor://localhost,tauri://localhost,http://tauri.localhost,https://tauri.localhost",
      },
    },
  },
}
